{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790369336,"nonce":"0sWjSvLqCHEsZFj27Eb/yTsaw3w=","title":"Lua: metafields such as __index publish as classes and statics","message":"A Lua module's interface is every string-keyed field of the table its chunk returns (zyntax_lua::exports, and at run time the host's `names` helper). In the common class idiom the metatable's own fields are among them:\n\n    local Point = {}\n    Point.__index = Point\n    function Point.new(x, y) return setmetatable({x = x, y = y}, Point) end\n    function Point:len() ... end\n    return Point\n\nHere `__index` holds a table of functions, so the module publishes a class named `__index` (and `Point.__index` is a static when Point is a class of a module table). Haxe then gets a type `lua.point.__index`.\n\nKeys that begin with `__` are Lua's metatable protocol (`__index`, `__call`, `__name`, ...), not the module's API. The rule belongs with the frontend's conventions, as Python's `_`-prefixed names do in zyntax_python::exports: zyntax_lua::exports leaves such fields out, or marks them, and caribou-lua's run-time extras follow the same rule. It was the same before the interface came from the types; this is not a regression.","files":null}]}